GreenBoost third_party/llama.cpp — vendored third-party code
===============================================================

Upstream project: llama.cpp
Repository:       https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp
Upstream commit:  e8f19cc0ad70a243c8012bf17b4be601abfc8ea2 (2026-07-16)
License:          MIT License (see LICENSE, verbatim from upstream)
Import date:      2026-07-24

What was imported
------------------
CMakeLists.txt, CMakePresets.json, cmake/, src/, include/, ggml/, common/,
tools/, vendor/, gguf-py/, conversion/, grammars/, requirements/, scripts/,
convert_hf_to_gguf.py, LICENSE, licenses/ — verbatim, from
https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp at the pinned commit.

NOT vendored: models/ (test-fixture GGUFs), docs/, tests/, benches/,
examples/, pocs/, app/, media/ — this repo's own tests cover the
GreenBoost-side integration instead (same exclusion policy as
synapse_engine/NOTICE). `LLAMA_BUILD_TESTS`/`LLAMA_BUILD_EXAMPLES`/
`LLAMA_BUILD_APP` are passed =OFF at configure time by
gb_synapse.build_engine() since these excluded directories are exactly
what those CMake options gate — they default ON in a standalone build
(LLAMA_STANDALONE), so the OFF flags are load-bearing, not cosmetic:
without them cmake fails looking for the missing add_subdirectory() paths.

`scripts/` was NOT in the original prune list and had to be added after a
real `cmake --build` against the pruned tree failed — `tools/ui` (part of
`llama-server`) needs `scripts/ui-assets.cmake` to build its embedded web
UI. Verified 2026-07-24: a full `cmake --build` of all four targets
(llama-server, llama-cli, ggml-rpc-server, llama-quantize) against this
exact pruned tree succeeds and all four binaries run.

Local modifications
--------------------
Every local patch to the vendored tree MUST be appended below, in order,
so future contributors can tell upstream code from GreenBoost's own
changes at a glance.

- 2026-07-24: Initial import, no source modifications yet.

- 2026-08-20: Per-request speculative draft depth.
  * tools/server/server-schema.cpp , took `speculative.n_max` out of the
    `#if 0` block upstream uses to disable per-request speculative
    parameters. Only that one field: `n_min`, `p_min` and `type` are read
    from the common_speculative object's params, which are fixed at init,
    so accepting them per request would parse cleanly and do nothing.
  * tools/server/server-context.cpp , `server_slot::get_n_draft_max()` now
    clamps by `task->params.speculative.draft.n_max`. Without this the
    request's value is overwritten by the context-fit bound on every step.
  Both changes can only LOWER the depth below the server's own
  `--spec-draft-n-max`; `common_speculative_draft()` already takes
  `min(params.n_max, dp.n_max)` and the target output buffer is sized from
  the server-level maximum, so nothing can overrun.
  Why: draft depth is the only decode lever left on a box whose weights do
  not fit in VRAM, and the right depth follows live acceptance rather than a
  constant (2026-08-05 sweep: tok/s non-monotonic in depth). Previously
  changing it meant restarting the server and discarding every warm slot.
